From 451049d9eaecbb548f891479eb016b4b8aca5024 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:54:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] policy routing: the rule list leaves nvram (1 KB kernel cap =
 ~15 rules)

Same wall the Gatekeeper device list hit at 45 devices (v2.6.3): the HND
kernel nvram store rejects any tuple over WLCSM_NAMEVALUEPAIR_MAX (1024)
silently - even a RAM-only set - and the libnvram whitelist is a blob. A
16th routing rule "saved" and vanished.

- shared/reaper_jfile.c: the gk_rl store shape made generic (rjf_get /
  rjf_set: atomic tmp+fsync+rename, 0600) + reaper_pbr_rl_get(), the one
  resolver both rc and httpd use: staged draft > confirmed list > legacy
  nvram value.
- The confirmed list IS /jffs/reaper_pbr/lastgood/rulelist (it was already
  the commit-confirm rollback source); an apply stages the draft in
  /tmp/reaper_pbr/draft (httpd writes it, never nvram); Keep promotes it;
  revert/rollback and a boot simply drop the draft. nvram keeps only
  reaper_pbr_enable.
- One-shot migration at boot: an nvram-held list seeds the file, then the
  nvram copy is cleared (and a leftover from a settings restore is dropped)
  so nothing stale shadows the file - exactly as gk_rl.
- Page: the list comes from reaper_pbr.cgi?action=cfg, not SSI.

Host test (fake nvram, mock iptables): migration log + nvram cleared,
arm/rollback/confirm unchanged, a 20-rule 1,222-byte list survives
apply+confirm and emits 20 mark rules.
